ody that says I'm not going well  look you said this best earlier we were  talking in Florida like in Louisiana  people have got a lot of experience with  storms unfortunately that can sometimes  lead to complacency yeah sometimes  people think it didn't hit me last time  it's not going to hit me this time the  second thing I would say in addition to  over preparing it's always better to ask  forgiveness not permission  sometimes the bureaucracy gets in the  way again I give you an example we had  commercial buses to help us evacuate New  Orleans they didn't show up in time they  kept telling us they were coming they  were coming  we actually got National Guardsmen some  of them who didn't have their commercial  driver's licenses  we got school buses we've got those  folks out there are many times where  FEMA they mean well but they can get in  the way they you may remember they were  telling people you can't go in the water  and rescue people without proof of  insurance and registration  cajun  didn't-didn't follow those rules they  saved a lot of people the third thing  and I would say this is the people of  Florida the first two were for a  government the third is for the people  of Florida you're the best first  responder for your neighbors and friends  the reality is look the local officials  the state officials of federal officials  are gonna do amazing jobs our first  responders will run towards danger not  away from it even when their own houses  are being flooded but if you've got a  neighbor if you've got a relative that  needs help  you're the best person to help that but  so get your family safe if you can help  a neighbor or an elderly relative please  do that the Cajun Navy the Volunteer  Force there in Louisiana on the subject  of response the Trump administration is  diverting 151 million dollars from FEMA  to border initiatives what do you make  of that look I think the important thing  is the resources need to be there for  Puerto Rico for Florida 